.TH "SOPLOS-SYS-CLEANER" "1" "2026-11-17" "Soplos Linux" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
soplos-sys-cleaner \- System cleaner and maintenance tool for Soplos Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B soplos-sys-cleaner
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B soplos-sys-cleaner-basic
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B soplos-sys-cleaner
scans the system for reclaimable space, shows exactly what it found and what
each item is, and only removes what the user confirms. Built with Tauri 2,
Rust, and Svelte, it is installed as two programs that share one history:
.IP \[bu] 2
.B soplos-sys-cleaner-basic
is the one in the applications menu: a health ring and a single standard
cleanup that only touches what is safe to remove. It asks for the
administrator password at most once.
.IP \[bu]
.B soplos-sys-cleaner
is the advanced program, with every page. It is opened from the basic
program, which can also add a launcher for it to the applications menu.
.P
Only one of them runs at a time. Everything cleaned from one shows up in the
Overview of the other.
.P
Every item is labeled
.BR Safe ,
.B Review
or
.BR Protected .
Review items are never preselected and are never part of the quick or the
full cleanup. Before any package is removed, a dry run
.RB ( "apt-get -s" )
shows everything that would go with it, and the cleanup is blocked if it would
remove something the system needs.
.P
The advanced program covers:
.IP \[bu] 2
.B APT package cache,
listed file by file so single
.B .deb
files can be picked. The
.BR apt (8)
command deletes its files after installing while
.BR apt-get (8)
keeps them, so the cache is often empty after updating with
.BR apt .
.IP \[bu]
.B Old kernels and modules.
The running kernel and the newest other kernel are always protected, and
kernels installed by hand are never treated as leftovers.
.IP \[bu]
.B Drivers and firmware
not in use, detected from the dracut configuration, PCI and USB devices,
loaded modules and the kernel log. A firmware family stays protected while its
driver is installed, and nothing is offered as unused if the detection tools
are missing.
.IP \[bu]
.B Orphaned packages
(what
.B apt autoremove
would remove) and
.B residual configuration
(packages removed but left their configuration files, dpkg state
.BR rc ),
always as Review, with a dry run first.
.IP \[bu]
.B Languages,
grouped per language, always keeping the active language and English, and
.B documentation
the user does not need.
.IP \[bu]
.B Flatpak
runtimes without use and
.B Snap
old revisions.
.IP \[bu]
.B User cache,
temporary files, the trash and crash dumps.
.IP \[bu]
.B System journal,
with retention by time or by size and an estimate of what it frees.
.IP \[bu]
.B Snapshots
of Timeshift (in its rsync and its BTRFS mode) and of Snapper, always as
Review. Reading them needs the administrator password, so nothing is listed
until the page asks for it with a button. The newest snapshot of each tool, and
of each Snapper configuration, is locked, so there is always a point to go back
to. The page can also make a snapshot now, with a short comment.
.IP \[bu]
.B Disk space,
in the Analysis group of the sidebar: where the space goes, folder by folder,
the largest first, for the home of the user or for the whole system, and how
much is free on the disk. It only shows: it has no delete button, because what
to free is decided in the folders.
.IP \[bu]
.B Installed apps
from APT, Flatpak, Snap and AppImage, each with its source, with export of a
reinstall script. APT, Flatpak and Snap apps are installed again by command;
AppImages are listed with their download address.
.IP \[bu]
.B History
with a receipt for every cleanup (kind, duration, command, items and result)
and export to CSV.
.IP \[bu]
.B Alerts.
The user chooses when to be notified: time since the last cleanup, size ready
to clean, or both. It only sends a desktop notification and never cleans by
itself. It is off by default, and while it is off there is no service.
.P
The window never runs as root. Everything that needs administrator rights goes
through a single Polkit-gated helper that receives named operations, never
scripts, and validates each one again as root before running it. Several
operations travel in one
.BR pkexec (1)
call, so one password covers a whole cleanup.
.P
The interface is available in English, Spanish, German, French, Italian,
Portuguese, Romanian and Russian, with light and dark themes. It starts in the
system language and falls back to English.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show summary of options.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Run the alerts check without opening a window: read-only, and it sends a
notification only if a cleanup is due. Started by the alerts timer.
.TP
.B \-\-notify \fITITLE\fR \fIBODY\fR
Send a single desktop notification and exit. Used by the test button of the
alerts page, through systemd.
.TP
.B \-\-run-ops \fIFILE\fR
Internal Polkit-authenticated task: validate and run the named operations
stored in
.IR FILE .
Used by the application itself through
.BR pkexec (1)
and not meant to be called by hand.
.SH KEYBOARD SHORTCUTS
.TP
.B F1
About dialog.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Q
Quit the application.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Tab, Ctrl+Shift+Tab
Next or previous section (advanced program).
.TP
.B F5, Ctrl+R
Rescan.
.TP
.B Ctrl+A
Select all or none (cleaning pages).
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+A
Select only the Safe items (cleaning pages).
.TP
.B Ctrl+Enter
Clean the selection (cleaning pages).
.TP
.B Ctrl+I
Show or hide the notes (advanced program).
.TP
.B Ctrl+E
Export the history as CSV.
.P
Shortcuts do nothing while a dialog is open, and Enter alone never confirms a cleanup.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/polkit-1/actions/org.soplos.syscleaner.policy
Polkit policy defining the administrator privileges for cleanup operations.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/soplos-sys-cleaner/stats.json
Cleanup history and statistics, shared by both programs.
.TP
.I ~/.config/soplos-sys-cleaner/alerts.json
Alerts settings. Present only after the alerts are configured.
.TP
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/soplos-sys-cleaner-alerts.service
.TQ
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/soplos-sys-cleaner-alerts.timer
Systemd user units of the alerts. Created when the alerts are turned on and
removed when they are turned off.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/applications/soplos-sys-cleaner-advanced.desktop
Launcher of the advanced program, if the user added it to the applications menu.
